+NOMROM 1.10.6+27 — Selected files inside a TorBox collection ZIP
+Copyright (c) Mark Mateo. Distributed under the repository's MIT license.
+
+EVIDENCE AND PURPOSE
+
+The user's 1.10.5+26 diagnostic report identifies a cached, finished torrent
+whose provider file list contains one explicitly zipped file:
+Minerva_Myrient.zip, 4,208,534,228 bytes. The selected catalog member is
+No-Intro/Nintendo - Super Nintendo Entertainment System/Super Mario World
+(USA).zip, 345,695 bytes. The report records validated torrent identity and
+available catalog proof, but the provider's member count and total size do
+not match the original collection. Ordinary filename matching cannot find
+an individual game when the provider exposes only that enclosing ZIP.
+
+The report does not reveal the ZIP's contents or its download server's range
+behavior. This update adds support for inspecting that representation safely;
+it does not assume the selected game is necessarily inside the returned ZIP.
+
+SELECTIVE ARCHIVE DOWNLOAD
+
+The existing ordinary-file resolver runs first. After a not-found or
+size-mismatch result, a separate container route is permitted only for one
+provider file explicitly marked zipped:true, a validated torrent identity,
+a bounded positive provider size, a valid provider file ID, and a complete
+catalog proof bound to the selected hash/index/path/positive-size tuple.
+The existing file ID is requested with zip_link=false. A collection's outer
+compressed size is not treated as the sum of its uncompressed catalog files.
+
+The catalog link, game filename and game size remain the durable download
+identity. The collection URL, size and transfer plan stay in memory. The URL
+does not replace the catalog link in JSON, task storage or resumed downloads.
+
+The archive reader requests bounded HTTP byte ranges. It requires status 206,
+the exact requested Content-Range and total length, identity encoding, and
+the exact response byte count. Redirects, requests, timeouts and metadata are
+bounded. A server that ignores Range fails explicitly; there is no fallback
+that downloads the whole collection. Private per-request cancellation also
+aborts the underlying connection when a response or range is rejected; the
+task's own cancellation token remains reserved for a user or lifecycle stop.
+Strong ETags use If-Match when provided;
+usable Last-Modified validators are checked when appropriate. Representation
+changes fail rather than combining incompatible ranges. When no strong
+validator is available, fixed range bounds, matching local/directory metadata,
+the exact output length and CRC32 remain mandatory. CRC32 is an integrity
+check, not a cryptographic proof of publisher identity.
+
+ZIP32 and ZIP64 metadata are supported, including member offsets beyond 4 GiB.
+The directory is limited to 16 MiB and 100,000 entries. The selected member
+must match a unique catalog path and size. A basename-only match also requires
+the complete directory's regular-file count and summed uncompressed bytes to
+match the catalog, plus proven catalog filename/size uniqueness. Literal plus
+and percent characters remain literal; ZIP names are not URL-decoded.
+
+Stored and Deflate members are supported. Split archives, encrypted entries,
+unsafe paths, links/special files, ambiguous members, inconsistent headers,
+invalid ranges and unsupported selected compression methods fail explicitly.
+Only the selected member's compressed byte range is streamed. Its output must
+match the catalog's exact size and an independently computed CRC32 before it
+is committed. Existing extraction and library handling then process that game
+file as usual. Large collections are not extracted into the library.
+
+LIFECYCLE AND DIAGNOSTICS
+
+Pause, cancellation and app-update preparation cancel archive work, drain
+pending writes and remove owned temporary files. Publication is guarded by
+the active task identity. Failed or interrupted archive work preserves an
+existing destination. A resumed Deflate member starts again from that member's
+beginning, not from an uncompressed byte offset in the outer ZIP. It never
+restarts a whole-collection download. Expired download links have a bounded
+re-resolution path using the original catalog tuple.
+
+Queue requests that arrive while another queue pass is running are coalesced
+into a further pass. This prevents an immediate Resume after app startup from
+being lost while the initial empty-queue check is still finishing.
+
+Archive failures retain the existing bounded, sanitized Copy details report
+with an archive-member stage and specific ZIP error code. Collection URLs,
+provider directories, account details, response bodies and credentials remain
+excluded. A failure such as ZIP_RANGE_UNSUPPORTED or ZIP_MEMBER_NOT_FOUND now
+identifies a different cause from ordinary provider FILE_NOT_FOUND.
+
+VERIFICATION AND LIMITS
+
+The release report records the actual automated test, native Windows build,
+package, updater-regression and native-smoke results. Fixtures use synthetic
+archives and local HTTP servers, including the reported provider wrapper
+shape. No game content, personal TorBox credentials or authenticated live
+download URL was used. Actual acceptance against the user's cached ZIP and
+an interactive installer upgrade remain separate from those checks.
+
+The original updater public configuration, database schema, Windows features,
+themes, attribution and prior Skraper integration remain in this candidate.
+Building and packaging do not publish a release or modify the signed feed.
